    "ground_truth_answer": "Certainly! Here are three concise recommendations to address the WiFi dead zones upstairs in your cousin's two-story house:\n\n1. **Upgrade or Reposition the Router/Extender**: \n   - Place a dual-band router (or an existing one if it supports both 2.4GHz and 5GHz) near the center of the house to maximize coverage.\n   - Consider using a mesh WiFi system or a Wi-Fi extender on the second floor to boost signal strength and eliminate dead zones.\n\n2. **Use Ethernet Backhaul for Extenders**:\n   - If using an extender, connect it via Ethernet to the main router (if possible) rather than relying solely on wireless backhaul. This ensures more stable and faster communication between devices.\n\n3. **Adjust Antenna Orientation**:\n   - Ensure that any external antennas on the router or extender are vertically oriented and elevated as high as possible (e.g., mounted on a wall or ceiling). This helps in better signal propagation throughout the house, especially upstairs.\n\nImplementing these suggestions should significantly improve WiFi coverage on the second floor.",
